lxrosser@sloth.ugcs.caltech.edu (Alex Rosser) writes:

>I'm working on compiling Nethack on my 486 PC running MSDOS. Can
>anyone recommend a relatively painless way to convert UNIX style
>return characters to an MSDOS carriage return? Many thanks,

One way might be to just load each file into any msdos editor, save it
and hope the editor does it.

Or, since you are compiling it, you obviously have a C compiler and 
you probably know some C.. it isn't very hard to write a program that
takes two filenames as arguments, reads the first file character by 
character and writes the second one adding the CR:s. Something like this:
(I didn't try this or even compile it, I just wrote it online, so there
may be some syntax errors or something..)


#include <stdio.h>

main (int argc, char *argv[])
{
 FILE inp, outp;
 char c;

 if (argc!=2) {
    printf ("I need two filename arguments");
    return 1;
 }
 if (!(inp=fopen(argv[1],"rb"))) {
    printf ("Can't open %s for input",argv[1]);
    return 1;
 }
 outp=fopen(argv[2],"wb");
 c= fgetc(inp);

 while (!feof(inp)) {
    if (c='\n')
	fputc ('\r',outp);
    fputc(c,outp);
    c= fgetc(inp);
 }

 fclose (inp);
 fclose (outp);
 return 0;
}

